Notification unload regression test
===================================

Run against a private installed Wine runtime containing the winmm and mmdevapi
changes and winepipewire.drv. The test needs Python 3, a MinGW compiler,
PipeWire, pw-cli and pw-metadata. It builds its own PE probe, creates a new Wine
prefix and starts a private PipeWire server with two virtual outputs. Desktop
audio and existing prefixes are not touched.

From this directory:

  python3 notification-unload.py --runtime /path/to/private/wine --arch x86_64
  python3 notification-unload.py --runtime /path/to/private/wine --arch i386

Use --work /path/to/new/directory to retain logs at a chosen location, --cc to
select the MinGW compiler, or --cycles to change the default 200 stress cycles.
Wine loaders and Unix libraries must belong to this private runtime, rather
than being symlinks which select a different installation.

The focused check holds the loader lock on the application thread, changes
the default output and waits for winmm's device mapping to follow it. Updating
that mapping requires winmm's notification callback to get past its TRACE.
Tracing is enabled deliberately: without the debug-helper initialization
before registration, the callback's first string formatting can wait for the
loader lock. The check fails with that initialization removed. It releases
the lock even on failure so the callback can finish before DLL cleanup.

The following unload/reload cycles are supplementary stress coverage. They do
not force each unload to overlap an active callback. This test does not prove
that arbitrary application callbacks can safely block or unregister clients.
The runner fails on a nonzero exit or a timeout; logs remain in its printed
output directory. The probe opens no audio stream and does not test WOM_DONE.
